1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,329
You have already met every failure mode in this lecture, once each, across the last

2
00:00:05,329 --> 00:00:06,169
four sections.

3
00:00:06,169 --> 00:00:11,088
This is the moment they get names, so the next time one shows up in your own work,

4
00:00:11,088 --> 00:00:15,947
you recognise it immediately instead of starting from confusion the way you might

5
00:00:15,947 --> 00:00:16,847
the first time.

6
00:00:16,847 --> 00:00:18,646
Why do names matter this much?

7
00:00:18,646 --> 00:00:21,885
Because an unnamed failure gets debugged from scratch.

8
00:00:21,885 --> 00:00:26,564
Picture the afternoon without the name: the report is wrong, so you reread the

9
00:00:26,564 --> 00:00:31,423
prompt, tweak a sentence, rerun, still wrong, add another sentence, and two hours

10
00:00:31,423 --> 00:00:36,402
later you have a longer briefing and the same wrong report, because the cause was a

11
00:00:36,402 --> 00:00:40,182
moved row and no briefing on earth reads a moved row correctly.

12
00:00:40,182 --> 00:00:44,981
Now the same afternoon with the name: the report is wrong, the facts check fails

13
00:00:44,981 --> 00:00:50,019
against the source, that is the drift signature, open the sheet, find the edit, four

14
00:00:50,019 --> 00:00:50,499
minutes.

15
00:00:50,499 --> 00:00:53,079
A name is a compressed checklist of causes.

16
00:00:53,079 --> 00:00:56,978
That compression is what this lecture hands you, five times over.

17
00:00:57,528 --> 00:01:00,981
Notice the last row does not describe the agent failing.

18
00:01:00,981 --> 00:01:03,570
It describes you failing to keep checking.

19
00:01:03,570 --> 00:01:08,626
That is deliberately on this list, because it is the single most common cause of a

20
00:01:08,626 --> 00:01:13,558
small mistake becoming a real one, across every industry that has ever automated

21
00:01:13,558 --> 00:01:14,113
anything.

22
00:01:14,113 --> 00:01:18,491
Attach a first move to each name and the table becomes a repair manual.

23
00:01:18,491 --> 00:01:23,546
Suspect invention: take the suspect fact and hunt for it in the source, and if the

24
00:01:23,546 --> 00:01:26,074
hunt comes up empty you have your answer.

25
00:01:26,074 --> 00:01:30,637
Suspect scope creep: stop reading outputs entirely and go read access, the

26
00:01:30,637 --> 00:01:35,631
credential grants and the folders, because creep lives in permissions, not prose.

27
00:01:35,631 --> 00:01:40,625
Suspect drift: open the source next to an old output and compare shapes, headers,

28
00:01:40,625 --> 00:01:42,536
orderings, anything structural.

29
00:01:42,536 --> 00:01:47,160
Suspect tone: read it aloud, the test that has not changed since Section 6.

30
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:51,908
Suspect over-trust: check one date, the last time you actually read an output

31
00:01:51,908 --> 00:01:55,669
properly, and let the answer embarrass you into a spot check.

32
00:01:55,669 --> 00:02:00,108
Five suspicions, five first moves, none of them longer than ten minutes.

33
00:02:00,658 --> 00:02:04,838
This is worth knowing before you spend your limited checking time.

34
00:02:04,838 --> 00:02:10,030
Spend more of it on the agents whose failure modes actually cost something if they

35
00:02:10,030 --> 00:02:13,450
slip through, not evenly across all four out of habit.

36
00:02:13,450 --> 00:02:18,769
The pattern generalises to every agent you will ever adapt, and it compresses to two

37
00:02:18,769 --> 00:02:20,352
questions about surfaces.

38
00:02:20,352 --> 00:02:22,632
Who else touches this agent's input?

39
00:02:22,632 --> 00:02:27,572
A shared sheet, a team inbox, anything other hands edit, means drift exposure,

40
00:02:27,572 --> 00:02:31,371
because other people's normal use moves your agent's ground.

41
00:02:31,371 --> 00:02:33,524
And who reads this agent's output?

42
00:02:33,524 --> 00:02:38,717
The moment the answer includes anyone outside your own desk, a client, a director,

43
00:02:38,717 --> 00:02:41,503
tone and invention start costing real money.

44
00:02:41,503 --> 00:02:43,403
Input shared, watch for drift.

45
00:02:43,403 --> 00:02:46,316
Output outbound, watch for tone and invention.

46
00:02:46,316 --> 00:02:51,002
Neither, and you can afford the lighter end of every sampling rate in C07.

47
00:02:51,002 --> 00:02:56,132
Those two questions, asked at design time, are how you place your checking before

48
00:02:56,132 --> 00:02:57,905
anything has gone wrong yet.

49
00:02:58,455 --> 00:03:03,312
Hold the distinction loosely for now and let Section 10's story plant it properly,

50
00:03:03,312 --> 00:03:08,288
because it lands differently once you have watched a careful colleague hit it on his

51
00:03:08,288 --> 00:03:13,264
very first week with a proven template, which is exactly how the course delivers it.

52
00:03:13,264 --> 00:03:18,122
The reason it earns a preview here is the pattern it completes: invention fails by

53
00:03:18,122 --> 00:03:22,209
adding, drift fails by the world moving, ambiguity fails by the input

54
00:03:22,209 --> 00:03:23,868
underdetermining the answer.

55
00:03:23,868 --> 00:03:27,600
Three different repairs, which is the whole argument for names.

56
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:31,332
And expect a seventh, eventually, that no course named for you.

57
00:03:31,332 --> 00:03:36,248
When it arrives, do what this lecture did: describe it precisely, name it, attach a

58
00:03:36,248 --> 00:03:37,907
first move, and add the row.

59
00:03:37,907 --> 00:03:41,521
The taxonomy is a tool you now own, not a list you memorised.

60
00:03:42,071 --> 00:03:47,164
That is next section, and it closes the loop this course opened back in Section 1.3

61
00:03:47,164 --> 00:03:48,821
with the word blast radius.

62
00:03:48,821 --> 00:03:53,792
Before you go there, one small assignment that makes this lecture stick: pick the

63
00:03:53,792 --> 00:03:58,763
one failure mode your most important agent is most exposed to, write its name and

64
00:03:58,763 --> 00:04:03,550
its first move on the same sticky note as your trial tallies, and put it where

65
00:04:03,550 --> 00:04:04,716
Monday will see it.

66
00:04:04,716 --> 00:04:07,416
Naming a failure before it happens is cheap.

67
00:04:07,416 --> 00:04:10,178
Meeting it unnamed is the afternoon you lose.

68
00:04:10,178 --> 00:04:15,210
And if you ever want to feel how much this section has changed you, try explaining

69
00:04:15,210 --> 00:04:19,567
one of these names to a colleague who just installed their first agent.

70
00:04:19,567 --> 00:04:22,513
The look on their face is you, six sections ago.
